&lt;h2 id=&quot;final-notes&quot;&gt;Final Notes&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Due to the compatibility issue between Kramdown and Mathjax, You should not use the condition symbol (&lt;code class=&quot;language-plaintext highlighter-rouge&quot;&gt;|&lt;/code&gt;) directly (kramdown table synyax), use the escaped text (&lt;code class=&quot;language-plaintext highlighter-rouge&quot;&gt;\vert&lt;/code&gt;) instead. Furthermore, you will want to use double dollar sign (&lt;code class=&quot;language-plaintext highlighter-rouge&quot;&gt;$$...$$&lt;/code&gt;) for most of your math notations, since single dollar sign (&lt;code class=&quot;language-plaintext highlighter-rouge&quot;&gt;$...$&lt;/code&gt;) may have some conflict with kramdown. (e.g., underscore need to be escaped with &lt;code class=&quot;language-plaintext highlighter-rouge&quot;&gt;\_&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;</content><author><name>Your Name</name><email>transverd@mit.edu</email></author><category term="math" /><category term="latex" /><summary type="html">In this document, we’ll showcase some commonly used features that Jekyll can achieve in a generated website. All of them can work out-of-the-box if you forked our Minimal Mistakes Template. The feature includes: markdown, mathjax, latex, yaml, css, html, liquid.</summary></entry></feed>